= Four Gentlemen Decoction (Sì Jūnzǐ Tāng, 四君子湯)

_The foundational qi-tonifying formula of Chinese medicine, and the base on which most of the later 補氣 formulary is built. Four drugs — *人參* (ginseng), *白朮* (atractylodes), *茯苓* (poria), and *炙甘草* (honey-fried licorice) — in equal parts, from the Song imperial formulary *太平惠民和劑局方* (1107–1110). It treats *脾氣虛 (Spleen-qi vacuity)*: fatigue, poor appetite, loose stools, a pale sallow complexion, a pale tongue with white coat, and a weak pulse. Its architecture is the formula's whole lesson — two drugs supplement, and two manage the dampness that supplementing a damp-prone organ would otherwise produce — so that it is cited throughout the tradition as the model of *補中有瀉*, drainage built into supplementation. Every ingredient here has, or will have, its own substance entry; the property summaries below are given in full so this entry can be read on its own._

== Name & Meaning

*四君子湯* — *四* (sì) "four" + *君子* (jūnzǐ) + *湯* (tāng) "decoction." The force of the name is in *君子*, the Confucian *gentleman* or *cultivated person*: the moral exemplar who governs by virtue and example rather than by coercion. Applied to pharmacology the metaphor is precise and deliberate — these four drugs are *平和 (pínghé,* mild and even-tempered), tonifying without attacking, achieving their effect by sustained support rather than by force. A formula of harsh purgatives would never be called a gathering of gentlemen.

A second reading runs underneath the first: in Chinese formula theory *君 (jūn)* is also the technical term for the *sovereign* drug, the chief of a prescription (see *君臣佐使* below). *四君子* can therefore be heard as "four drugs each fit to be sovereign" — four gentle tonics of such standing that any of them could lead a formula. Both readings are current in the commentarial tradition, and the pun is almost certainly intended.#footnote[*君子* in its Confucian sense: Analects _passim_; the term's semantic range ("gentleman," "noble person," "the cultivated one") is standard in Sinological usage. The pharmacological pun on 君 (sovereign drug) is noted in the formula-commentary tradition and in modern teaching texts; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed., discusses the naming. The reading given here presents both senses rather than choosing between them.]

*A homonym trap, flagged:* *四君子* also names the *"Four Gentlemen" of literati painting and poetry* — plum blossom, orchid, bamboo, and chrysanthemum — an entirely unrelated cultural trope sharing only the four-virtues metaphor. The formula is not made of those plants and has no connection to them. See `substances/chrysanthemum.typ`, where the same trap is flagged from the other side.

*Standard English renderings:* "Four Gentlemen Decoction," "Four-Gentleman Decoction," "Decoction of the Four Noble Ingredients," and in the patent-medicine trade "Four Major Herbs Combination." *Japanese (Kampo):* 四君子湯 *Shikunshitō*. *Korean:* 사군자탕 *sagunjatang*.

== Source & History

The formula's standard attribution is to the *太平惠民和劑局方 (Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng,* "Formulary of the Bureau of the Taiping Pharmacy for the People's Welfare"), the *Song imperial formulary*, compiled under state auspices and issued in the period *1107–1110* — the same source this book cites for the formula elsewhere (see `substances/fuling.typ`, `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`).

*On the attribution, honestly:* the Hejijufang is the earliest source in which the formula appears *under this name and in this composition*, but it is a *state compilation of existing practice* rather than an authorial invention, and the combination has clear antecedents. It is frequently observed that Four Gentlemen stands in a direct line from Zhang Zhongjing's *理中丸 (Lǐ Zhōng Wán,* Regulate the Middle Pill) — ginseng, atractylodes, licorice, and dried ginger — with *茯苓 replacing 乾薑*, which converts a formula for frank Spleen *cold* into one for Spleen *qi vacuity* with a damp-draining rather than a warming emphasis. Whether that derivation is historical or merely structural is not settled, and this entry does not assert it as a transmission claim.#footnote[_Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (太平惠民和劑局方), Song imperial formulary, 1107–1110 — the standard attribution; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. The structural relation to 理中丸 (_Shāng Hán Lùn_ / _Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_ lineage) is widely noted in the commentarial and teaching literature; whether it reflects actual derivation or retrospective comparison is flagged as unresolved here rather than asserted.]

*Its standing: a 基礎方 (jīchǔ fāng, "base formula").* Four Gentlemen is not principally a prescription to be given as it stands — though it is given — but *the structural parent of a family*. 異功散, 六君子湯, 香砂六君子湯, 參苓白朮散, 八珍湯, and 十全大補湯 are all built on it by addition, and 補中益氣湯 shares much of its logic. To learn this formula is to learn the grammar of the entire 補氣 category, which is why it is taught first.

*In modern practice* it remains in constant use across China, Japan, Korea, Taiwan, and Vietnam, chiefly in its expanded forms. Its Kampo derivative *六君子湯 (Rikkunshitō)* is among the most prescribed Kampo formulas in Japan and carries the best formula-level clinical research of the family (see Modern Pharmacology).

== Composition

*Classical proportions (Hejijufang):* the four drugs in *equal parts* — *各等分 (gè děng fēn)* — ground to a powder and taken as a *2 錢 (qián)* dose decocted in water. The equal-parts instruction is itself part of the formula's character: no drug dominates, which is the compositional expression of the "four gentlemen" idea.

*Modern clinical decoction doses* (reference figures requiring verification against current pharmacopoeial and clinical practice):
- *人參 (rénshēn,* ginseng) — *6–9 g* — or *黨參 (dǎngshēn,* codonopsis) *12–15 g* as the routine modern substitute.
- *白朮 (báizhú,* atractylodes rhizome) — *9–12 g*, usually *炒白朮* (stir-fried).
- *茯苓 (fúlíng,* poria) — *9–12 g*.
- *炙甘草 (zhì gāncǎo,* honey-fried licorice) — *3–6 g*.

Note that modern practice has shifted the proportions away from strict equality — the licorice is routinely reduced relative to the other three, both because it is the envoy rather than a principal and because of the glycyrrhizin ceiling discussed under Cautions.

*On role assignment, flagged:* the *君臣佐使* attributions below follow the most common teaching, with *人參* sovereign and *白朮* minister. Commentators differ — some treat 人參 and 白朮 jointly as a sovereign pair, others promote 茯苓 to minister alongside 白朮. The disagreement is real and interpretive; the *functional* account in Formula Architecture does not depend on resolving it.

=== 人參 (rénshēn, ginseng) — 君, sovereign

*Properties:* Sweet (甘), slightly bitter (微苦); slightly warm (微溫). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Lung (肺), Heart (心), Kidney (腎). *Principal actions:* 大補元氣 (greatly tonifies the source qi), 補脾益肺 (tonifies Spleen, augments Lung), 生津止渴, 安神益智.

*Function in this formula:* it supplies the formula's *primary tonifying force*, supplementing Spleen and Lung qi directly. Ginseng is chosen rather than a gentler qi tonic because the pattern addressed is *vacuity proper* — an actual deficit of qi, not merely its stagnation — and ginseng is the drug that fills a deficit fastest. Its Lung channel entry matters as well: the shortness of breath and weak voice of this pattern are Lung-qi signs, and ginseng reaches both organs at once.

*Why this drug rather than its neighbours:* *黃芪 (astragalus)* also tonifies Spleen-Lung qi but acts preferentially on the *surface* (衛氣) and on *raising* sinking qi — which is why substituting it produces a different formula (補中益氣湯) rather than a variant of this one. *黨參 (codonopsis)* is the accepted modern substitute, milder and much cheaper, and is what most prescriptions actually contain; it is weaker and *lacks ginseng's capacity in acute or severe vacuity*, so the substitution is an economic and gentleness decision, not an equivalence.

See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ` for the full treatment, including the drug-form system (紅參 / 白參) and the modern interaction profile that this formula inherits.

=== 白朮 (báizhú, Atractylodes macrocephala rhizome) — 臣, minister

*Properties:* Bitter (苦), sweet (甘); warm (溫). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃). *Principal actions:* 健脾益氣 (strengthens the Spleen, augments qi), 燥濕利水 (dries dampness, promotes urination), 止汗 (stops sweating), 安胎 (calms the fetus).

*Function in this formula:* it does two things at once, and the second is the reason it is here. It *strengthens the Spleen* alongside the sovereign — and it *dries dampness*. Because Spleen vacuity itself generates damp (*脾虛生濕*), a formula that only supplemented would leave the damp to accumulate; 白朮 addresses the consequence while the sovereign addresses the cause. The *stir-fried* form (炒白朮) is specified in practice because frying moderates its drying edge and brings forward the Spleen-strengthening action.

*Why this drug rather than its neighbours:* *蒼朮 (cāngzhú,* _Atractylodes lancea_) is the related rhizome and is *more strongly drying but far less tonifying* — appropriate where damp predominates and vacuity does not. Choosing 白朮 over 蒼朮 is precisely the statement that this is a *vacuity* formula with damp as a secondary consequence, not a damp formula.

_No substance entry yet for 白朮; the summary above is given in full for that reason, and the glossary carries the term._

=== 茯苓 (fúlíng, Poria) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Sweet (甘), bland (淡); neutral (平). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Heart (心), Kidney (腎). *Principal actions:* 利水滲濕 (promotes urination, leaches out dampness), 健脾 (strengthens the Spleen), 寧心安神 (calms the Heart, settles the spirit).

*Function in this formula:* it *drains the dampness that 白朮 dries*, by percolation through urination — the classical principle being *淡味滲泄*, "the bland flavour percolates and discharges." The *白朮 + 茯苓* pairing is the canonical dry-and-drain couplet of Chinese pharmacy, and the two are routinely found together for exactly this reason: one disperses damp in the tissues, the other provides it an exit.

Its second and less obvious function is *protective*: the other three drugs are all sweet, and sweet tonics are *滋膩* (cloying) — capable of producing the fullness and distension they were meant to relieve. 茯苓, bland and draining, keeps the supplementation moving. This is the mechanism of *補中有瀉* in miniature, and the same role 茯苓 plays among the "three drainers" of 六味地黃丸.

See `substances/fuling.typ` for the full treatment, including the five classical drug-forms and the 猪苓 contrast.

=== 炙甘草 (zhì gāncǎo, honey-fried licorice) — 使, envoy

*Properties:* Sweet (甘); neutral (平), tending warm in the honey-fried form. *Channels:* Heart (心), Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃). *Principal actions:* 補脾益氣 (tonifies the Spleen, augments qi), 調和諸藥 (harmonizes all the medicines), 緩急止痛 (moderates urgency, relieves pain).

*Function in this formula:* two roles, as the envoy position implies. It *adds to the Spleen-qi supplementation*, so it is not merely a corrective; and it *harmonizes* — 調和諸藥 is licorice's signature action across the entire pharmacy, moderating and binding the other drugs into a single coherent prescription rather than three simultaneous ones.

*The processing is not optional.* *炙* (honey-fried) licorice is warmer and more tonifying; *生甘草* (raw) is cooler and clears heat and toxicity. A formula built to warm and supplement the middle requires the honey-fried form, and substituting raw licorice changes the formula's thermal character. Processing form is part of composition, not a detail of sourcing.

See `substances/chinese-licorice.typ` and `substances/licorice-root.typ` for the full treatment — including the glycyrrhizin ceiling that governs this formula's safe duration of use.

== Formula Architecture

The pattern is *脾氣虛 (Spleen-qi vacuity)*, and the Spleen's governing function in this system is *運化 (yùnhuà,* transportation and transformation): it transforms food and fluid into qi and blood and distributes them. When that function fails, two things happen *simultaneously*, and the formula's whole design follows from the pairing:

1. *A deficit* — food is not transformed into qi, so the body is under-supplied: fatigue, weak voice, shortness of breath, pale complexion.
2. *An accumulation* — the untransformed residue becomes *damp*, which then further burdens the organ that failed to transform it. This is the classical maxim *脾虛生濕* (Spleen vacuity generates damp), and it describes a *vicious circle*, not a side effect.

A formula that only supplemented would feed the second problem while treating the first. Four Gentlemen therefore does both at once:

- *人參* and *炙甘草* — supplement the qi (treat the deficit).
- *白朮* — strengthens the Spleen *and* dries the damp (treats both, which is why it is the minister).
- *茯苓* — drains the damp outward (treats the accumulation, and keeps the sweet supplementation from stagnating).

This is the structure the tradition calls *補中有瀉* — *drainage within supplementation* — and Four Gentlemen is its teaching example, as *六味地黃丸* is for the yin-tonifying category with its three-tonics-and-three-drainers construction (see `substances/rehmannia.typ`, `substances/fuling.typ`).

*What breaks if an ingredient is removed* — the most direct way to see the argument:
- *Remove 茯苓* and the formula becomes cloying; in a patient with any tendency to fullness it produces epigastric distension, the classical *中滿* that sweet tonics are warned against.
- *Remove 白朮* and the damp is not dried; the formula supplements into a damp-encumbered middle and the circle is left intact.
- *Remove 人參* and the formula loses its force — it becomes a mild Spleen support rather than a treatment for vacuity proper.
- *Remove 炙甘草* and the three remaining drugs act in parallel rather than as one prescription, and the sweet-moderating, pain-easing quality is lost.

*The qi-and-blood symmetry.* Four Gentlemen has a deliberate counterpart in *四物湯 (Sì Wù Tāng,* Four Substances Decoction), the foundational *blood*-nourishing formula. The two are treated as a matched pair — 四君子 for qi, 四物 for blood — and their combination is itself a named formula, *八珍湯 (Bā Zhēn Tāng,* Eight Treasures), for dual qi-and-blood vacuity. That the tradition built its qi and blood foundations as a symmetrical pair is a good illustration of how systematically this formulary is constructed.#footnote[Pattern mechanism (運化, 脾虛生濕), the 補中有瀉 reading, and the 四君子湯 / 四物湯 / 八珍湯 relation: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; standard modern formula-theory teaching texts. The "remove an ingredient" analysis is the conventional pedagogical treatment of this formula, not an original argument.]

== Indications & Pattern

*Pattern:* *脾胃氣虛 / 脾氣虛弱* — Spleen (and Stomach) qi vacuity, without marked cold and without marked damp-heat.

*Presenting signs:*
- *食少 (shí shǎo)* — reduced appetite; early satiety.
- *大便溏薄* — loose, unformed stools; sometimes undigested food in the stool.
- *倦怠 / 四肢無力* — lassitude and weakness of the limbs; fatigue disproportionate to exertion.
- *語音低微, 氣短* — a weak, low voice and shortness of breath on exertion (the Lung-qi component).
- *面色蒼白 / 萎黃* — a pale or sallow-yellow complexion.
- *Tongue:* pale body with a thin white coat (舌淡苔白).
- *Pulse:* weak, thin, or soft and moderate (脈虛弱 / 細緩).

*Modern correlates — stated as correlations, not equivalences:* the pattern is commonly met in chronic gastritis and functional dyspepsia, chronic diarrhoea and IBS-type presentations, convalescence after illness or surgery, chemotherapy-associated gastrointestinal upset and fatigue, and general debility in the elderly. *These are the conditions in which the pattern is often found, not diagnoses the formula treats* — the prescription follows the pattern, and a patient with functional dyspepsia presenting with damp-heat or Liver-qi constraint needs a different formula entirely.

*Differentiation from adjacent formulas* — the clinically load-bearing part of any formula entry:
- *理中丸 (Regulate the Middle Pill)* — same Spleen focus but *frank cold*: cold limbs, abdominal pain relieved by warmth, watery diarrhoea. It contains 乾薑 where this formula has 茯苓. *Four Gentlemen is for vacuity without marked cold.*
- *補中益氣湯 (Tonify the Middle and Augment Qi)* — for Spleen-qi vacuity with *sinking*: prolapse, chronic diarrhoea, bearing-down sensation, low-grade fever from vacuity. 黃芪 is sovereign and lifting drugs (升麻, 柴胡) are added. *Four Gentlemen has no raising action.* See `substances/astragalus.typ`.
- *參苓白朮散* — Four Gentlemen's own derivative, for when *damp and diarrhoea dominate* the picture rather than accompanying it.
- *六君子湯* — Four Gentlemen plus 陳皮 and 半夏, for vacuity *with phlegm-damp*: nausea, vomiting, copious sputum.
- *四物湯* — the *blood* analogue; where the presentation is pallor with dizziness, palpitations, and scanty menstruation rather than fatigue with poor appetite.

== Modifications & Derived Formulas

This section is the main justification for treating Four Gentlemen as an entry in its own right: the formula is used less often as itself than as a base.

*Named derived formulas, in order of expansion:*
- *異功散 (Yì Gōng Sǎn,* Extraordinary Merit Powder) — *+ 陳皮* (tangerine peel). The minimal expansion: adds qi-regulating and aromatic transformation so that supplementation does not stagnate. For Spleen vacuity with mild distension or poor appetite. From Qian Yi's *小兒藥證直訣* (1119). See `substances/tangerine-peel.typ`.
- *六君子湯 (Liù Jūnzǐ Tāng,* Six Gentlemen Decoction) — *+ 陳皮 + 半夏*. Folds the logic of *二陳湯* (the base phlegm-damp formula) into Four Gentlemen: for Spleen vacuity *with phlegm-damp* — nausea, vomiting, cough with copious thin sputum. The most clinically used member of the family.
- *香砂六君子湯 (Xiāng Shā Liù Jūnzǐ Tāng)* — *+ 木香 + 砂仁* on top of Six Gentlemen. Adds aromatic qi-moving for epigastric and abdominal *distension and pain*.
- *參苓白朮散 (Shēn Líng Bái Zhú Sǎn)* — *+ 山藥, 蓮子, 白扁豆, 薏苡仁, 砂仁, 桔梗*. Shifts the emphasis decisively toward *damp and chronic diarrhoea*; the 桔梗 is there to lift and to open the Lung, giving the formula an upper-body reach the base lacks. See `substances/coix.typ`.
- *八珍湯 (Bā Zhēn Tāng,* Eight Treasures Decoction) — *四君子湯 + 四物湯*. For *dual qi-and-blood vacuity*. See `substances/dang-gui.typ`, `substances/rehmannia.typ`.
- *十全大補湯 (Shí Quán Dà Bǔ Tāng,* All-Inclusive Great Tonifying Decoction) — *八珍湯 + 黃芪 + 肉桂*. Deeper dual vacuity with cold; the most supplementing formula of the series. See `substances/astragalus.typ`.
- *人參養榮湯 (Rén Shēn Yǎng Róng Tāng)* — a further expansion for qi-blood-yin vacuity with consumption. See `substances/schisandra.typ`.

*Common modifications (加減) to the base:*
- *+ 黃芪* — for more marked qi vacuity, spontaneous sweating, or a tendency to catch colds.
- *+ 當歸* — for concurrent blood vacuity (and the step toward 八珍湯).
- *+ 乾薑* or *+ 附子* — for a cold component (and the step back toward 理中丸).
- *+ 山藥, 蓮子* — for loose stools and chronic diarrhoea.
- *+ 砂仁, 木香* — for distension.
- *+ 麥芽, 神曲, 山楂* — where food stagnation accompanies the vacuity.
- *黨參 for 人參* — the routine modern substitution; milder and cheaper, at larger dose.

*Kampo note:* *六君子湯 (Rikkunshitō)* is among the most frequently prescribed Kampo formulas in Japan and is the member of this family with the strongest formula-level clinical evidence — see Modern Pharmacology. Japanese practice standardizes composition and dose in a way Chinese practice does not, which is part of why the research is more tractable there.#footnote[Derived formulas and modifications: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; Qian Yi, _小兒藥證直訣_ (Xiǎo Ér Yào Zhèng Zhí Jué), 1119, for 異功散. Kampo prescribing patterns: Japanese Kampo clinical literature — verify current prescribing statistics.]

== Preparation & Administration

*Classical method (Hejijufang).* The four drugs in *equal parts*, ground to a powder; a *2 錢 (qián)* dose is decocted in roughly a cupful of water, reduced by about a third, and drunk warm, *not tied to mealtimes*. The classical weight 錢 is approximately *3.7 g* in late-imperial measure, so a 2-qián dose is on the order of *7–8 g of combined powder* — strikingly small by modern decoction standards, and a reminder that the classical delivery was a powder-decoction, not a large raw-herb brew. *The exact wording of the Hejijufang instruction should be verified against a specific edition* rather than taken from this entry.

*Modern decoction (湯劑) — the usual form today:*
1. Combine the herbs at the modern doses given under Composition.
2. *Soak* in cold water 20–30 minutes before heating.
3. Bring to a boil, then simmer gently — the standard practice is *two successive decoctions*, the liquids combined and divided.
4. Divide into *two doses per day*, taken *warm*.

*Timing.* Tonic formulas are classically given *before meals or on an empty stomach*, so that the supplementation is not competing with the digestion of food. *There is a real divergence here:* for patients whose digestion is markedly weak — which is, after all, this formula's own indication — many practitioners prefer administration *after* meals to avoid burdening an empty, vacuous middle. Both practices are current; the choice follows the patient.

*Handling the sovereign.* Ginseng being costly and its actives sensitive to hard boiling, it is commonly *另煎 (lìng jiān,* decocted separately) and the liquid combined with the rest, or prepared by *隔水燉* (double-boiling in a covered vessel set in water). Where 黨參 is substituted, it is simply decocted with the others. See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`.

*Course.* This is a *cumulative* prescription, given over weeks rather than days; the classical expectation is gradual restoration, and a formula of this kind judged on three days' effect has not been given a chance to work.

*Modern prepared forms.* Granules (顆粒 / "科學中藥"), pills, and patent preparations are all available and widely used. *Flag:* granule concentration ratios vary by manufacturer and are *not interchangeable gram-for-gram* with raw-herb decoction; follow the specific product's labelling rather than converting from decoction doses.#footnote[Preparation method, the 錢 weight conversion (~3.7 g in late-imperial measure, with regional and period variation), and modern decoction practice: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; standard Chinese pharmacy references. The classical Hejijufang instruction is paraphrased here as to its shape (equal parts, powdered, small dose decocted and drunk warm, not tied to mealtimes) and flagged for verification against a specific edition rather than quoted. Granule-to-decoction non-equivalence: manufacturer labelling and modern Chinese pharmacy practice.]

== Cautions & Contraindications

*Pattern contraindications (classical):*
- *Excess and repletion patterns, and acute exterior illness.* A warm, sweet, supplementing formula given during an acute febrile or exterior condition reinforces the pathogen — the classical rule against *補其實* (supplementing what is already full).
- *Damp-heat.* The sweet warmth feeds damp-heat; this formula is for vacuity with plain damp, not with heat.
- *Yin vacuity with heat signs.* The warming sweet tonics aggravate.
- *中滿 (middle fullness).* Marked epigastric or abdominal distension, or frank food stagnation, is the cardinal caution against sweet-cloying tonics. The existence of 異功散 and 六君子湯 is the tradition's own answer to it: where fullness is present, add the qi-movers rather than giving the base.

*Ingredient-derived cautions — these are inherited in full and are the practical limits on the formula:*
- *炙甘草 / glycyrrhizin — the governing modern caution for this formula.* Licorice at sustained dose causes a *pseudoaldosteronism* picture: sodium and water retention, *hypokalaemia*, oedema, and *raised blood pressure*. This sets a real ceiling on the duration and dose at which any licorice-containing formula should be taken, and it is the main reason modern practice reduces the licorice below equal parts. *Caution or avoid* in hypertension, oedema, hypokalaemia, congestive heart failure, and renal impairment; *interactions* with diuretics (additive potassium loss), corticosteroids, and digoxin (hypokalaemia potentiating digoxin toxicity). See `substances/licorice-root.typ`, `substances/chinese-licorice.typ`.
- *人參 — the ginseng interaction profile applies unchanged:* reduced INR reported with *warfarin*; additive hypoglycaemia with *antidiabetic medication*; case reports with *MAO inhibitors*; additive overstimulation with *stimulants and caffeine*; theoretical opposition to *immunosuppressants*; and caution in *uncontrolled hypertension*. Classically, *人參反藜蘆* (十八反) and *人參畏五靈脂* (十九畏). See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`.
- *Folk rule, (F)-tier:* ginseng-containing formulas are traditionally not taken with *tea* or *radish/daikon*, both held to disperse the qi being tonified.

*Pregnancy — an honest tension.* The formula's minister, *白朮*, is classically *安胎* (fetus-calming), and Spleen-vacuity formulas of this family are used in pregnancy in traditional practice. But modern safety data for *ginseng* in pregnancy are insufficient, and for the licorice component prolonged use is undesirable. The classical and modern readings pull in different directions here; the honest position is that this is a decision for a qualified practitioner rather than a rule this entry can state.

*Reference information, not personalized medical advice.* Formula selection, modification, and dosing in Chinese medicine are properly a practitioner's work; the glycyrrhizin and ginseng cautions above are the two that most often matter in practice and both warrant clinical oversight for anything beyond short-term use.#footnote[Pattern contraindications and 中滿: standard formula-theory sources; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. Glycyrrhizin pseudoaldosteronism, its dose-duration relation, and the diuretic/corticosteroid/digoxin interactions: see `substances/licorice-root.typ` and the current herb-drug interaction literature — this is a well-characterized and clinically real effect, not a theoretical one. Ginseng interactions: see `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`. All modern cautions require current verification.]

== Modern Pharmacology

*State the evidence situation first, because it is easy to misrepresent:* formula-level research is far thinner than the research on the individual herbs, and *the constituent herbs' literature is not evidence for the compound*. Most of what exists for this family concerns the derivative *六君子湯 (Rikkunshitō)* rather than Four Gentlemen itself.

- *六君子湯 / Rikkunshitō — the best-evidenced member.* Randomized trials in *functional dyspepsia* and in *chemotherapy-induced nausea and anorexia* report benefit, and a mechanism has been proposed and studied in some depth: *potentiation of ghrelin signalling*, with effects on gastric accommodation and emptying. This is unusually good evidence for a classical multi-herb formula and is largely a product of Japanese Kampo's standardization of composition and dose.
- *四君子湯 itself* — the literature is predominantly *Chinese-language, preclinical, and small-trial*: effects reported on gastrointestinal motility, intestinal barrier function and mucosal protection, immunomodulation, and more recently gut-microbiota composition; small clinical studies in chronic gastritis, chemotherapy-associated gastrointestinal toxicity, and post-operative recovery. *Methodologically heterogeneous and mostly not independently replicated* — read as suggestive, not established.
- *Mechanistic plausibility* for the classical indication is reasonable at the level of gastrointestinal motility and mucosal function, which is approximately what *運化* describes in modern terms — but the correspondence should be treated as a useful analogy rather than a demonstrated identity.
- *What has not been shown:* that the formula does anything for fatigue, immunity, or recovery beyond what its gastrointestinal effects would predict; and that the four-herb combination outperforms its components, which is the specific claim the formula's architecture makes and which the literature does not currently test.#footnote[Rikkunshitō (六君子湯) randomized trials in functional dyspepsia and chemotherapy-induced nausea/anorexia, and the ghrelin-signalling mechanism: Japanese Kampo clinical and pharmacological literature — verify against current systematic reviews. 四君子湯 preclinical and clinical literature (gastrointestinal motility, barrier function, immunomodulation, microbiota): predominantly Chinese-language, small-scale and heterogeneous — flagged accordingly, consistent with this book's standard on formula-level evidence. The absence of combination-versus-components testing is stated as an absence, not an argument against the formula.]

== Sources

- _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (太平惠民和劑局方), Song imperial formulary, 1107–1110 — the source text; the classical composition, equal-parts instruction, and powder-decoction method.
- Qian Yi, _Xiǎo Ér Yào Zhèng Zhí Jué_ (小兒藥證直訣), 1119 — 異功散.
- Zhang Zhongjing, _Shāng Hán Lùn_ / _Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_ — 理中丸, for the structural comparison.
- Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press) — the principal modern reference for composition, architecture, differentiation, derived formulas, and preparation.
- Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. — for the single-drug properties summarized here.
- _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ (current edition) — monographs for 人參, 白朮, 茯苓, 甘草 and their processed forms.
- Japanese Kampo clinical and pharmacological literature on 六君子湯 (Rikkunshitō) — functional dyspepsia, chemotherapy-induced nausea, ghrelin signalling; verify against current systematic reviews.
- Current herb-drug interaction literature for glycyrrhizin (pseudoaldosteronism, diuretic/corticosteroid/digoxin interactions) and for ginseng (warfarin, antidiabetics, MAOIs).
- Substance entries for the ingredients: `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`, `substances/fuling.typ`, `substances/chinese-licorice.typ`, `substances/licorice-root.typ`; and for the derived-formula cross-references `substances/tangerine-peel.typ`, `substances/coix.typ`, `substances/astragalus.typ`, `substances/rehmannia.typ`, `substances/dang-gui.typ`, `substances/schisandra.typ`. No substance entry yet exists for *白朮* (atractylodes); its properties are summarized in full above and the term is in `zz-glossary.typ`.
- Foundational frameworks: `climates.typ`, `tibb-al-arabi.typ`, `zz-glossary.typ`.
